  "captions": [
    {
      "startFrame": 0,
      "endFrame": 30,
      "text": "Did you know"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 30,
      "endFrame": 66,
      "text": "Germany has nine neighbors —"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 66,
      "endFrame": 102,
      "text": "more than any other EU country?"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 102,
      "endFrame": 138,
      "text": "Let's count them:"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 138,
      "endFrame": 183,
      "text": "Denmark, Poland, Czechia,"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 183,
      "endFrame": 228,
      "text": "Austria, Switzerland, France,"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 228,
      "endFrame": 282,
      "text": "Luxembourg, Belgium and the Netherlands."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 282,
      "endFrame": 345,
      "text": "But its strangest borders are tiny."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 345,
      "endFrame": 420,
      "text": "On the Rhine, near Schaffhausen,"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 420,
      "endFrame": 495,
      "text": "lies Büsingen — a German village"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 495,
      "endFrame": 570,
      "text": "completely surrounded by Switzerland."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 570,
      "endFrame": 645,
      "text": "It's only 700 meters from the rest of Germany,"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 645,
      "endFrame": 720,
      "text": "yet it pays in Swiss francs"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 720,
      "endFrame": 810,
      "text": "and sits inside Swiss customs territory."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 810,
      "endFrame": 885,
      "text": "Germany also traps pieces of Austria."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 885,
      "endFrame": 975,
      "text": "The Kleinwalsertal valley is Austrian,"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 975,
      "endFrame": 1065,
      "text": "but mountains cut it off from the rest of Austria."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 1065,
      "endFrame": 1155,
      "text": "The only road in comes from Oberstdorf, in Germany."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 1155,
      "endFrame": 1245,
      "text": "Nearby, the Austrian village of Jungholz"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 1245,
      "endFrame": 1335,
      "text": "is almost completely surrounded by Germany."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 1335,
      "endFrame": 1440,
      "text": "It touches Austria at just one point:"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 1440,
      "endFrame": 1560,
      "text": "the summit of the Sorgschrofen mountain."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 1560,
      "endFrame": 1650,
      "text": "But the strangest border of all is near Aachen."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 1650,
      "endFrame": 1740,
      "text": "After World War I, a railway called the Vennbahn"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 1740,
      "endFrame": 1830,
      "text": "was handed to Belgium — along with the land under it."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 1830,
      "endFrame": 1920,
      "text": "That thin Belgian strip runs right through Germany,"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 1920,
      "endFrame": 2010,
      "text": "cutting off five pieces of German land."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 2010,
      "endFrame": 2100,
      "text": "They became German enclaves surrounded by Belgium."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 2100,
      "endFrame": 2190,
      "text": "The largest is the village of Mützenich,"
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 2190,
      "endFrame": 2295,
      "text": "where crossing the old railway means leaving Germany."
    },
    {
      "startFrame": 2295,
      "endFrame": 2400,
      "text": "The trains are gone — it's a bike path now — but the border remains."
    }
  ],
